      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d=⟨$  \n\n{{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}},\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{5}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-6+5}{1+4+25}\\,c \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{30}\\,⟨-1,2,5⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟨-1,2,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{6},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{5}{6}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
